    exiting. The spec now has the handler log and then [38;5;153mprocess.exit(1)[39m deliberately, and uses [38;5;153maddEventListener[39m in the
    browser rather than assigning [38;5;153mwindow.onerror[39m, which would have clobbered any existing handler.
  - [1m[38;5;153mError[39m objects would have logged as [38;5;153m{}[39m.[0m [38;5;153mmessage[39m and [38;5;153mstack[39m are non-enumerable, so the redaction walk would have thrown
    away the only useful part. The spec now converts errors to [38;5;153m{ name, message, stack }[39m (following [38;5;153mcause[39m) before
    redacting.
  - [1mAdded a risk[0m that the synchronous-exit crash path will silently drop records once an async remote sink exists — a
    trap for whoever implements the transport later.

  One thing I'd flag as unresolved rather than solved: the spec logs usernames plainly and defines no retention policy
  for them. That's a defensible call for debugging, but it means production logs will accumulate PII, and where those
  logs eventually get shipped is a decision this design explicitly defers.

  Please review the spec and tell me if you want changes. Once you approve it, the next step is the writing-plans skill
  to turn it into an implementation plan — I won't write any code before then.

